        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            A broadcast spreader looks like the simplest tool in the garage: a hopper, a hole, a spinning disc. But it determines whether a bag of fertilizer greens your lawn evenly or leaves it striped, whether seed germinates in a uniform carpet or in patches, and whether a winter of ice melt destroys the machine. We picked spreaders by focusing on the parts that decide those outcomes.
          </p>

          <p>
            We started with what homeowners and professionals actually buy and can get parts for: Scotts for consumer spreaders, LESCO and Earthway for commercial-grade walk-behinds, Agri-Fab for tow-behinds, and Chapin for winter work. Each was compared on hopper capacity, spread width and evenness, how the rate gate is controlled, wheel type, frame material, gearbox protection and how easy it is to find the correct setting for the product you are applying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Settings availability matters more than most reviews admit. Scotts spreaders have settings printed on almost every Scotts bag, which makes them the easiest for beginners. Commercial spreaders from LESCO and Earthway rely on manufacturer charts or on your own calibration, which is more accurate once done but a real step up in effort. We rated both approaches on their merits rather than assuming either was better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Durability was the other key test. Fertilizer and ice melt are both salts, and salt corrodes steel, seizes axles and eats cheap gearboxes. We read through long-term owner reviews looking specifically at failure points — rusted axles, stripped gears, stuck gates and cracked hoppers — and weighted build quality heavily for anyone planning to also spread ice melt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, every spec here comes from the manufacturer or consistent retail listings. Where a figure was unclear, we left it out. We do not quote exact prices, which change weekly; we describe each spreader as budget, mid-range or premium instead.
          </p>
