        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Broadcast vs Drop vs Handheld Spreaders
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are three main types of lawn spreader, and choosing the right one matters more than choosing between brands.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Broadcast (rotary) spreaders</h3>

          <p>
            A broadcast spreader drops product onto a spinning impeller, which throws it in a fan several feet wide. This is the right choice for almost every lawn. It covers ground quickly, the edges of the pattern taper off so overlapping passes blend together, and it is forgiving of slightly wandering lines. The downside is precision: product lands beyond the edge of the lawn unless you use an edge shield, and the pattern can be uneven on windy days or with light materials like grass seed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Drop spreaders</h3>

          <p>
            A drop spreader releases product straight down through a row of holes between the wheels, so it only covers the width of the hopper. It is extremely precise, which makes it good for lawns bordered by gardens, ponds or streams where stray fertilizer does damage. It is also slow, and because the edges of each pass are hard lines, missing by a couple of inches leaves a visible stripe and overlapping leaves a dark one. For most homeowners, a broadcast spreader with EdgeGuard gives most of the precision with far less effort.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Handheld spreaders</h3>

          <p>
            Handheld spreaders, crank or battery-powered, are for small areas: patches, narrow side yards, beds and steps. They are the best tool for spot-seeding bare patches with <a href="/best-grass-seed/">grass seed</a>, and a useful companion to a push spreader. They are not a substitute for one on a full lawn.
          </p>

