        <div id="edgeguard" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. EdgeGuard and Edge Control
          </h2>

          <p>
            The biggest weakness of any broadcast spreader is the edge. The impeller throws product several feet to both sides, so when you walk along a driveway, sidewalk or flower bed, half your pattern lands where it should not. On concrete, that means fertilizer stains, wasted product and granules washing into storm drains. On a bed, it means feeding weeds or burning ornamental plants with a weed and feed product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Scotts' EdgeGuard is the best-known fix. On the Elite, DLX and Mini, a lever on the handle drops a shield that blocks the right side of the throw. You walk the perimeter with the lawn on your left and the edge on your right, the shield stops product crossing onto the driveway, and the lawn edge still receives product. It is simple, it works, and it is the main reason we recommend Scotts spreaders to beginners.
          </p>

          <p>
            Commercial spreaders handle edges differently. The Earthway 2150 has adjustable side-throw control, and the LESCO has a built-in deflector. These work, but they need more attention than flipping a lever. Tow-behind spreaders, with their very wide throw, generally have no effective edge control; plan to do the perimeter with a push or handheld spreader.
          </p>

          <p>
            The correct technique regardless of spreader is to do the perimeter first. Walk two header strips around the whole lawn with the edge control engaged. Then fill in the middle with back-and-forth passes, shutting the gate off as you reach the header strip at each end. This gives the turnarounds somewhere to land and prevents the doubled-up dark green stripes that appear where you turn.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Perimeter first, then the middle.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Two header strips around the edge with EdgeGuard on, then parallel passes through the center with the gate closed during every turn.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Edge control matters most with herbicide products. A granular <a href="/best-weed-killer/">weed killer</a> or weed and feed thrown into a flower bed can damage ornamentals, and on a pre-emergent product it can stop desired seeds from germinating. Always check the label for keep-away distances.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="calibration" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Spreader Settings and Calibration
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every bag of lawn product lists a rate, usually in pounds per thousand square feet, and most list a setting for common spreaders. The setting is the number on the rate dial that opens the hopper gate enough to put down the correct rate at a normal walking pace. Get it wrong in one direction, and you waste product and can burn the lawn with fertilizer; get it wrong in the other, and the product does nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            With Scotts spreaders, calibration is mostly done for you. Scotts prints settings on its bags for its own spreaders, and its website has a lookup tool for other brands' products. Walk at the pace Scotts assumes — a steady, normal walking pace — and the setting will be close. If you walk much faster or slower, the rate changes, because the gate opening is fixed but the ground speed is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            With commercial spreaders, or with products that do not list your spreader, calibrate yourself. The simple method: mark out 1,000 square feet of driveway or lawn, weigh a known amount of product into the hopper, spread it at your normal pace, and weigh what is left. The difference is your rate per thousand square feet at that setting. Adjust and repeat until it matches the bag. It takes 20 minutes and it is the single best thing you can do for even results.
          </p>

          <p>
            A widely used professional trick is to set the spreader to half the recommended rate and cover the lawn twice, in perpendicular directions. It takes twice as long, but it virtually eliminates striping because any unevenness in one pass is filled in by the other. For expensive products like starter fertilizer before seeding, or when using a <a href="/best-fertilizer-for-grass/">slow-release fertilizer</a> where uneven coverage shows for months, it is worth the extra time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Always close the gate when you stop, turn or stand still. Product keeps flowing when the gate is open, and a spreader standing still for ten seconds leaves a dead patch. Check the calibration every season, too; gates and linkages drift, and cable-operated controls stretch.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hopper" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Hopper Capacity and Lawn Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            Spreader capacity is quoted two ways, and the difference catches people out. Scotts quotes coverage — the hopper holds enough product for 5,000, 15,000 or 20,000 square feet — while commercial brands quote weight, like 50 lb or 80 lb. Coverage is more useful for homeowners, but it assumes a typical Scotts fertilizer; heavy products like ice melt or light products like seed will fill the hopper differently.
          </p>

          <p>
            The goal is to finish your lawn on one or two fills. Refilling is not just an inconvenience: it is where most mistakes happen, because you set the spreader down, spill product and restart in the wrong place. Measure your lawn before you shop. Pace off the length and width of each section and multiply; subtract the house, driveway and beds. Most suburban lawns are smaller than their owners think.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">COVERAGE PER FILL</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2,500</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">sq ft, Scotts Wizz</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5,000</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">sq ft, EdgeGuard Mini</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20,000</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">sq ft, Scotts Elite</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~1 acre</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Agri-Fab 130 lb tow</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            As a rough guide: under 5,000 square feet, the EdgeGuard Mini is all you need. From 5,000 to about 15,000, the DLX or the Elite. From 15,000 to half an acre, the Elite or a 50 lb commercial walk-behind like the Earthway. Beyond half an acre, an 80 lb LESCO if you prefer to walk, or a tow-behind if you own a tractor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bigger is not free. A full 80 lb hopper is heavy to push up a slope, and a large hopper on a small lawn means leftover product sitting in the spreader. Never store product in the hopper: fertilizer absorbs moisture, cakes and corrodes the gate. Empty the hopper back into the bag, then rinse and dry the spreader after every use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="build" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Pneumatic Tires, Stainless vs Plastic
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two build details separate spreaders that last one season from spreaders that last ten: the tires and the materials the frame and gearbox are made from.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tires</h3>

          <p>
            Pneumatic, air-filled tires, like those on the LESCO, Earthway and Agri-Fab tow-behind, are the best for spread accuracy. They absorb bumps, so the hopper does not jolt and the impeller throws a steady pattern. They also roll more easily on soft or lumpy ground. The downside is that they can go flat. Foam-filled tires, like the Elite's, never go flat and still roll reasonably well. Hard plastic wheels are the cheapest and the worst: they bounce over every bump, the pattern varies with each jolt, and they slip on wet grass.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Frame and gearbox</h3>

          <p>
            Fertilizer and ice melt are both salts, and salt corrodes steel quickly. Painted steel frames rust wherever the paint chips, and axles and impeller shafts seize. That is why commercial spreaders like the LESCO use stainless steel for the frame, axle and impeller shaft. Plastic frames and hoppers, like the Scotts models, do not rust at all, but they flex, crack and wear, and plastic gears strip under load. For homeowner use a few times a year, plastic is fine. For heavy use, or year-round use including ice melt, stainless is worth the premium.
          </p>

          <p>
            An enclosed gearbox matters as much as the frame. Open gears collect grit and fertilizer dust, which grinds them down. All of the commercial spreaders here, and the Agri-Fab tow-behind, use enclosed gear housings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance evens out a lot of these differences. Rinse the spreader after every use, including the underside and the impeller, let it dry completely, and store it indoors. A drop of oil on the axle and gate pivots each season keeps it moving. A well-kept plastic spreader will outlive a neglected steel one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ice-melt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Using a Spreader for Ice Melt
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most broadcast spreaders are marketed for fertilizer, seed and ice melt, and a spreader is far better than a scoop for putting down an even, economical layer of ice melt on a long driveway. But there are good reasons to think twice before using your lawn spreader in winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Salt is the problem. Rock salt and most ice melts are highly corrosive, far more so than fertilizer, and they get into gears, axles and gate linkages. Ice melt also absorbs moisture and clumps, which jams gates and agitators. A lawn spreader used for a winter of ice melt and not rinsed carefully will usually show it by spring: a seized wheel, a stuck gate or a rate that no longer matches the setting.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a precision issue. On a driveway, you want the spread to stay on the pavement. A lawn spreader's wide fan throws salt onto the turf and beds beside the driveway, and salt kills grass. The browned strip along many driveways every spring is salt damage, not winter kill.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best answer, if you use a lot of ice melt, is a dedicated winter spreader like the Chapin 8003A, with baffles that aim the spread onto the pavement and a spiked auger that breaks up clumps. If you only have one spreader, choose one with stainless components or plastic construction, engage the edge shield when working along the lawn, rinse it thoroughly after every use, and dry it before storage. Pair it with a product designed for your surface; our guide to the <a href="/best-ice-melt-for-concrete/">best ice melt for concrete</a> covers which products are safest on newer driveways.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WINTER RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">One spreader for the lawn, one for the salt.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you salt more than a handful of times a year, a dedicated winter spreader pays for itself by keeping your lawn spreader corrosion-free and your turf salt-free.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="tow-behind" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When a Tow-Behind Spreader Makes Sense
          </h2>

          <p>
            A tow-behind spreader hitches to a lawn tractor, garden tractor or zero-turn and does the walking for you. On a large property it is transformative: the Agri-Fab 45-0463 covers roughly an acre on one fill and throws up to 12 feet, so an acre that takes well over an hour on foot takes a fraction of that from the seat.
          </p>

          <p>
            The threshold where a tow-behind starts to make sense is about half an acre, assuming you already own a tractor. Below that, the time saved does not justify the storage space, and the wide throw is hard to control on a small lot. Above an acre, it becomes the obvious choice. If you do not already own a tractor, a tow-behind spreader is not a reason to buy one; a LESCO or Earthway commercial walk-behind covers an acre comfortably on foot. If you are already shopping for a tractor, our <a href="/best-riding-lawn-mower/">riding lawn mower guide</a> covers models with a proper hitch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tow-behind spreaders need a slightly different technique. Ground speed changes the rate, so pick the steady ground speed your manual recommends and stick to it. Open and close the gate using the control rod as you reach the end of each pass. Because the throw is so wide, measure the effective spread width, which is usually well under the maximum, and space your passes accordingly.
          </p>

          <p>
            The edges will need separate treatment. A 12-foot throw along a driveway puts product on the concrete, so do the perimeter with a push spreader with EdgeGuard, or a handheld, then fill the middle with the tow-behind. It sounds like extra work, but it only takes a few minutes and it keeps fertilizer out of storm drains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tow-behinds are also a natural fit for large-scale seeding. After core aeration with a tow <a href="/best-lawn-aerator/">lawn aerator</a>, a tow-behind spreader puts seed and starter fertilizer into the holes across the whole property in one trip, and the cores and seed get dragged together into the soil.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Seeding, Feeding and Timing: Getting Even Results
          </h2>

          <p>
            A good spreader gives you the ability to spread evenly; technique is what makes it happen. These habits matter most.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prepare the lawn before you spread. For fertilizer, the granules need to reach the soil, not sit on top of leaves or thatch. In fall, clear leaves first, with a <a href="/best-lawn-sweeper/">lawn sweeper</a> on larger lawns, before a fall feeding. For seeding, dethatch and aerate first; seed that lands on a thatch layer rarely reaches the soil and fails to germinate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Match the product to the season. Most cool-season lawns benefit most from fertilizer in early fall and again in late fall, with a lighter feeding in spring. Our <a href="/best-fertilizer-for-grass/">fertilizer guide</a> covers which formulas suit which season. For overseeding, early fall is the best window in the north, because soil is warm and weeds are slowing down. Pick a <a href="/best-grass-seed/">grass seed</a> matched to your sun and region, and set the spreader using the seed bag's rate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spread on a still, dry day. Wind distorts the pattern, especially with light seed. For most granular fertilizers, spread on dry grass and then water in lightly, unless the label says otherwise. For granular weed and feed products, the opposite is often true: they need to stick to wet weed leaves, so apply to damp grass in the morning. Always read the label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Walk at a steady pace, overlap correctly and never stand still with the gate open. On a broadcast spreader, overlap each pass so the edge of one throw just reaches the wheel track of the previous pass. That gives the tapered edges room to blend. Use the half-rate, two-direction technique for expensive or critical applications.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean up as you go. Blow or sweep any granules off hard surfaces back onto the lawn. Fertilizer left on the driveway washes into storm drains and stains concrete, and herbicide on the patio can be tracked indoors. Rinse the spreader, let it dry, and store it with the gate closed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the gate open while turning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every time you stop, turn or pause with the gate open, product piles up in one spot and leaves a burned patch or a dark green blotch. Close the gate before each turn and reopen it as you start walking. Doing header strips around the perimeter first gives you room to turn without missing ground.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the setting without checking your pace</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bag settings assume a steady, normal walking pace. Walk slower and you over-apply; walk faster and you under-apply. If you tend to stroll, or you are using a tow-behind, calibrate on a measured 1,000 square foot area rather than relying on the printed number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing product in the hopper</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fertilizer and ice melt absorb moisture, cake solid and corrode the gate and axle. Leaving even a small amount in the hopper between applications is the most common way spreaders fail. Pour leftover product back into the bag, rinse the spreader including the impeller, and let it dry before storing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the lawn spreader for salt all winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ice melt is far more corrosive than fertilizer, and it clumps. A lawn spreader used for a winter of salt often has a seized wheel or stuck gate by spring. If you salt regularly, buy a dedicated winter spreader, or at minimum rinse and dry your lawn spreader after every use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spreading onto driveways and beds</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Granules on concrete wash into storm drains and stain, while weed and feed thrown into flower beds damages ornamentals. Use EdgeGuard or a side deflector for the perimeter, and blow any stray granules off hard surfaces back onto the lawn before watering in.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best broadcast spreader for homeowners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners with lawns up to about half an acre, the Scotts Elite is the best choice. Its dual rotors give a wider, more even pattern than single-rotor spreaders, EdgeGuard keeps product off edges, and settings are printed on every Scotts bag. For smaller lawns or a tighter budget, the EdgeGuard DLX or Mini do the same job with fewer features.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a Scotts spreader with other brands of fertilizer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Many non-Scotts fertilizer bags print a setting for common Scotts spreaders, and Scotts has an online lookup tool. If you cannot find a setting, calibrate by spreading a weighed amount over a measured 1,000 square foot area and adjusting until the rate matches the bag. The spreader itself works with any granular product.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a drop spreader or broadcast spreader better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most lawns, a broadcast spreader is better. It covers ground much faster and its tapered pattern blends overlapping passes, so it is less likely to leave stripes. A drop spreader is more precise and suits lawns next to water, vegetable gardens or delicate beds. A broadcast spreader with an edge shield gives most of that precision with far less effort.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I avoid stripes when spreading fertilizer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Set the spreader to half the recommended rate and cover the lawn twice, walking the second pass perpendicular to the first. Overlap each pass so the edge of the throw reaches the previous wheel track, walk at a steady pace, and close the gate during every turn. Spreading on a calm day also helps keep the pattern even.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a broadcast spreader for ice melt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is much more even and economical than a scoop. But ice melt is corrosive and clumps, so rinse and dry the spreader after every use and never store salt in the hopper. If you salt frequently, a dedicated spreader like the Chapin 8003A with baffles will protect both your lawn spreader and the grass beside your driveway.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size spreader do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Measure your lawn first. Under 5,000 square feet, a compact spreader like the EdgeGuard Mini is enough. From 5,000 to 20,000 square feet, a full-size push spreader like the DLX or Elite. From about half an acre up, a 50 to 80 lb commercial walk-behind, or a tow-behind spreader if you own a lawn tractor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are pneumatic tires worth it on a spreader?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if your lawn is bumpy or you spread often. Air-filled tires absorb bumps, which keeps the hopper steady and the throw pattern consistent, and they roll more easily on soft ground. Foam-filled tires, like on the Scotts Elite, are a good compromise that never go flat. Hard plastic wheels are the least accurate on rough turf.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

